<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!-- =========================================================================
     CompLB3D  |  REFERENCE INPUT FILE
     =========================================================================

     This file is a TEMPLATE and a REFERENCE.  It lists every setting CompLB3D
     understands, says what each one does, gives its units and its default, and
     is set up as a small run that works out of the box.

     It is deliberately NOT a scientific example.  The uranium bioremediation
     case that used to live here has been moved to

         CompLaB_example_uranium_zhao.xml

     and is still complete and runnable - copy it over this file if that is the
     case you want.

     =========================================================================
     HOW TO READ THIS FILE

       A setting is a labelled box:  <name>value</name>  means "name = value".
       Boxes inside boxes group related settings.

       A COMMENT is a note for humans and the solver ignores it.  It opens with
       an angle bracket, an exclamation mark and two dashes, and closes with two
       dashes and an angle bracket.  This whole guide is one comment.

       CompLB3D reads this file from the directory you launch it in, and reads
       it by NAME: it must be called CompLaB.xml.

     =========================================================================
     UNITS - one convention, used everywhere

       lengths          micrometres by default (set <unit>)
       concentrations   mol/L
       biomass          mol/L   - the same unit as the chemicals, on purpose
       time             seconds
       diffusivities    m^2/s
       rate constants   per second   (they live in the .hh files, not here)

       Flux balance analysis is the one exception: it works internally in
       mmol per gram dry weight per hour, because that is what every published
       metabolic model uses.  CompLB3D converts at the boundary of the linear
       program using each microbe's <biomass_molar_mass>, and nowhere else.
       You never write mmol/gDW/h anywhere except <maximum_uptake_flux>.

     =========================================================================
     WHAT THIS TEMPLATE RUNS AS SHIPPED

       Flow plus transport of three tracers, no reactions.  That is a genuine,
       useful first run: it tells you the geometry loads, the flow converges,
       the Peclet auto-calibration works and the boundary conditions behave,
       before any chemistry can confuse the picture.

       To add chemistry, turn on ONE of the four reaction paths in
       <simulation_mode> below and read the note attached to it.  They can be
       combined; each note says what it needs from you first.

     =========================================================================
     WHAT YOU MUST SUPPLY

       input/geometry.dat   an ASCII list of material numbers, one per voxel,
                            written in x-slices: for each x from 1 to nx-2, all
                            (y,z) values.  The numbers are the ones you define
                            in <material_numbers> below.
     ========================================================================= -->
<parameters>

    <!-- ====================================================================
         WHERE THINGS LIVE
         ==================================================================== -->
    <path>
        <src_path>src</src_path>          <!-- source folder; also where Python looks for
                                               complab3d_cobrapy.py.  default: src        -->
        <input_path>input</input_path>    <!-- geometry.dat and any metabolic model XMLs.
                                               default: input                             -->
        <output_path>output</output_path> <!-- results are written here; created if absent.
                                               default: output                            -->
    </path>


    <!-- ====================================================================
         MASTER SWITCHES  -  what physics is active
         ====================================================================
         Everything here is a plain on/off.  Accepted spellings for "on":
         true, yes, 1, on.  Anything else is off.
         ==================================================================== -->
    <simulation_mode>

        <!-- Are there microbes at all?  false skips the whole microbiology
             section and runs pure chemistry.        default: true             -->
        <biotic_mode>true</biotic_mode>

        <!-- Hand-written biological rate laws from defineKinetics.hh.
             default: true

             BEFORE YOU TURN THIS ON: defineKinetics.hh as shipped is written
             for the 95-substrate uranium network and indexes C[0] .. C[86] by
             hard-coded position.  With the 3 substrates below it would read
             past the end of the array.  Either edit defineKinetics.hh to match
             your substrate list, or leave this off.                           -->
        <enable_kinetics>false</enable_kinetics>

        <!-- Chemical rate laws with no microbe involved (mineral reactions,
             redox, sorption) from defineAbioticKinetics.hh.   default: false
             Same warning as above: the shipped file expects the uranium
             substrate list.                                                   -->
        <enable_abiotic_kinetics>false</enable_abiotic_kinetics>

        <!-- ================================================================
             THE OPTIONAL METABOLIC LAYER
             ================================================================
             Three ways to let a genome-scale metabolic model decide how fast
             an organism grows and what it eats, instead of writing the rate
             law by hand.  All three are off by default and cost nothing when
             off.

             Each one ALSO has to be built into the program.  If you switch one
             on here and the program was compiled without it, CompLB3D stops at
             start-up and tells you which build option to turn on - it will not
             quietly ignore you.

                 GLPK      cmake -DENABLE_GLPK=ON ..
                 COBRApy   cmake -DENABLE_COBRAPY=ON ..
                 surrogate always available
             ================================================================ -->

        <!-- Flux balance analysis solved by GLPK.  Fast, in-process, no Python.
             This is the one to use for production runs.        default: false
             NEEDS: <model_filename>, <exchange_reaction_indices> and
                    <biomass_molar_mass> on each FBA microbe, plus a metabolic
                    model XML in input/ produced by  python3 extractMM.py       -->
        <enable_fba_glpk>false</enable_fba_glpk>

        <!-- The same analysis solved by COBRApy in an embedded Python.  Slower
             - typically one to two orders of magnitude - but it accepts the
             cobra ecosystem directly.  Useful for cross-checking GLPK results.
             default: false
             GLPK and COBRApy microbes cannot be mixed in one run.             -->
        <enable_fba_cobrapy>false</enable_fba_cobrapy>

        <!-- A pre-trained stand-in for flux balance analysis, defined in
             surrogateModel.hh.  Cheapest of the three, and only trustworthy
             inside the range of conditions it was trained on.  default: false -->
        <enable_surrogate>false</enable_surrogate>

        <!-- Which concentration the uptake bounds see when the equilibrium
             solver is on:

               free    the substrate lattice value as-is.  Reproduces CompLaB
                       2D exactly.                                  (default)
               total   the component plus every complex that carries it,
                       rebuilt from the <equilibrium> tableau, with consumption
                       drawn back down across the complexes in proportion.

             With <equilibrium> off the two are identical, because every
             substrate is then already its own total.  With speciation ON,
             "total" is almost certainly the physically correct choice: "free"
             will understate the available substrate, sometimes by a lot.      -->
        <fba_concentration_basis>free</fba_concentration_basis>

        <!-- Which GLPK algorithm.  1 = simplex (default and recommended),
             2 = interior point, 3 = exact rational arithmetic.  Leave at 1
             unless you have a specific reason.                                -->
        <glpk_lpsolver>1</glpk_lpsolver>

        <!-- Debugging only: dump each microbe's linear program to a file at
             start-up.  0 = off (default), 1 = .lp, 2 or 3 = .mps              -->
        <glpk_save_problem>0</glpk_save_problem>

        <!-- Per-iteration mass-balance and progress printing.  Verbose.
             default: false                                                    -->
        <enable_validation_diagnostics>false</enable_validation_diagnostics>

    </simulation_mode>


    <!-- ====================================================================
         THE GRID, THE FLOW, AND HOW LONG TO RUN
         ==================================================================== -->
    <LB_numerics>

        <domain>
            <nx>64</nx>          <!-- voxels along x; flow runs in +x.  REQUIRED -->
            <ny>32</ny>          <!-- voxels along y.                   REQUIRED -->
            <nz>32</nz>          <!-- voxels along z.                   REQUIRED -->
            <dx>2.0</dx>         <!-- voxel edge length, in <unit>.     REQUIRED -->
            <unit>um</unit>      <!-- m, mm or um.            default: um       -->

            <!-- The pore size that sets the Peclet number, in the same <unit>.
                 For a duct, the open width.  For a packing, a typical pore
                 throat.  REQUIRED whenever <Peclet> is nonzero.               -->
            <characteristic_length>24</characteristic_length>

            <!-- Material map, read from <input_path>.        REQUIRED         -->
            <filename>geometry.dat</filename>

            <!-- GENERATE the pore space instead of reading a file (new).
                 kind: channel, spheres, cylinders, random, fracture, layered.
                 The result is written out as a .dat so the run is reproducible
                 from its own output. Whatever the source, the geometry is
                 inspected before the first step: porosity, whether it
                 percolates inlet to outlet, and how much pore space is
                 isolated. A sealed domain stops the run, because a pressure
                 drop across one is not a well-posed problem. -->
            <!--
            <generate>random</generate>
            <porosity>0.35</porosity>
            <grain_radius>6</grain_radius>
            <grain_count>20</grain_count>
            <aperture>6</aperture>
            <roughness>2</roughness>
            <layers>4</layers>
            <seed>1</seed>
            <walls>y</walls>
            <write_geometry>generated_geometry.dat</write_geometry>
            -->

            <!-- Or IMPORT a raw binary volume, such as segmented CT data
                 exported from ImageJ. PNG and TIFF stacks are not read here;
                 tools/geometry.py handles those. -->
            <!--
            <import_raw>scan.raw</import_raw>
            <raw_dtype>uint8</raw_dtype>
            <threshold>128</threshold>
            <invert>false</invert>
            -->


            <material_numbers>
                <pore>2</pore>              <!-- open fluid.        default: 2  -->
                <solid>0</solid>            <!-- grain interior; no dynamics at
                                                 all.               default: 0  -->
                <bounce_back>1</bounce_back><!-- no-slip wall.      default: 1  -->

                <!-- One <microbeN> tag per ATTACHED microbe, giving the
                     material number its biofilm occupies.  A microbe with NO
                     tag here is treated as planktonic (free-floating) and is
                     advected with the flow instead.

                     This is the switch that decides attached versus suspended.
                     Several microbes may share a number.                      -->
                <microbe0>3</microbe0>
                <!-- microbe1 has no tag on purpose: it is the planktonic pool. -->
            </material_numbers>
        </domain>

        <!-- Pressure drop across the domain, in lattice units.  This is only a
             SEED: if <Peclet> is nonzero the solver measures the permeability,
             works out the pressure drop that hits your target Peclet, and
             re-solves.  Start small so the first solve is stable.
             default: 0 (no flow - pure diffusion)                            -->
        <delta_P>1.5e-4</delta_P>

        <!-- Target Peclet number: advection divided by diffusion, on the
             characteristic length.  1 means the two are comparable.  Set 0 for
             a diffusion-only run, which also skips the flow solver entirely.
             default: 0                                                        -->
        <Peclet>1.0</Peclet>

        <!-- Lattice-Boltzmann relaxation time for the FLOW.  Sets the model
             viscosity through nu = (tau - 0.5)/3.  Stay inside 0.5 < tau < 2.
             default: 0.8                                                      -->
        <tau>0.8</tau>

        <!-- Time the solver kernels instead of writing output.  Turning this on
             suppresses ALL VTI and checkpoint writing.       default: false   -->
        <track_performance>false</track_performance>

        <iteration>
            <ns_max_iT1>100000</ns_max_iT1>       <!-- flow solver step cap, first solve.  default: 100000 -->
            <ns_max_iT2>100000</ns_max_iT2>       <!-- ... and for later re-solves.        default: 100000 -->
            <ns_converge_iT1>1e-8</ns_converge_iT1> <!-- "flow is steady" tolerance, first. default: 1e-8  -->
            <ns_converge_iT2>1e-6</ns_converge_iT2> <!-- ... later.                         default: 1e-6  -->
            <ns_update_interval>1</ns_update_interval>  <!-- re-solve the flow at most every
                                                             N reaction steps.  default: 1  -->
            <ade_update_interval>1</ade_update_interval><!-- refresh diffusivities at most
                                                             every N steps.     default: 1  -->
            <ade_max_iT>20000</ade_max_iT>        <!-- reaction/transport steps to run.    default: 1e7 -->
            <ade_converge_iT>1e-8</ade_converge_iT> <!-- parsed but not currently used: there
                                                         is no transport convergence test.          -->
        </iteration>
    </LB_numerics>


    <!-- ====================================================================
         THE CHEMICALS
         ====================================================================
         One <substrateN> block per chemical, numbered from 0 with no gaps.
         The ORDER fixes the C[] indices that defineKinetics.hh and
         <exchange_reaction_indices> refer to, so do not reorder casually.
         
         OR NAME THEM, which is safer and is now recommended:
             <exchange_reaction_names>EX_ac_e EX_o2_e none</exchange_reaction_names>
         The indices above are POSITIONAL. If the model file is revised and one
         reaction is inserted, every index after it points somewhere else and the
         run does not complain. A name either resolves or the run stops with the
         name printed. Use none for a substrate this organism does not exchange.
         ==================================================================== -->
    <chemistry>

        <number_of_substrates>3</number_of_substrates>   <!-- REQUIRED -->

        <!-- Optional, one flag per substrate, only meaningful when an FBA
             solver is on:
               fix_concentration  hold this substrate at its current value; the
                                  reaction increment is computed but not applied
               fix_lower_bounds   clamp the uptake bound to exactly minus the
                                  local concentration
             Omit both unless you know you need them.                          -->
        <!-- <fix_concentration>0 0 0</fix_concentration>   -->
        <!-- <fix_lower_bounds>0 0 0</fix_lower_bounds>     -->

        <substrate0>
            <name_of_substrates>acetate</name_of_substrates>  <!-- used for the VTI file
                                                                   names and for matching
                                                                   equilibrium components -->
            <initial_concentration>0.0</initial_concentration><!-- mol/L.     default: 0  -->

            <substrate_diffusion_coefficients>
                <in_pore>1.0e-9</in_pore>       <!-- m^2/s in open water.  default: 1e-9  -->
                <in_biofilm>2.0e-10</in_biofilm><!-- m^2/s inside biofilm. default: 2e-10 -->
            </substrate_diffusion_coefficients>

            <!-- Dirichlet = hold this value fixed here (use it to inject).
                 Neumann   = free outflow, nothing imposed.
                 Both types are REQUIRED; the values default to 0.             -->
            <left_boundary_type>Dirichlet</left_boundary_type>
            <left_boundary_condition>5.0e-3</left_boundary_condition>
            <right_boundary_type>Neumann</right_boundary_type>
            <right_boundary_condition>0.0</right_boundary_condition>

            <!-- <immobile>true</immobile> marks a solid phase: it never
                 advects, diffuses, streams or collides, it only accumulates its
                 reaction term in place.  Use it for minerals and precipitates.
                 default: false                                                -->
        </substrate0>

        <substrate1>
            <name_of_substrates>sulfate</name_of_substrates>
            <initial_concentration>1.0e-3</initial_concentration>
            <substrate_diffusion_coefficients>
                <in_pore>1.0e-9</in_pore><in_biofilm>5.0e-10</in_biofilm>
            </substrate_diffusion_coefficients>
            <left_boundary_type>Dirichlet</left_boundary_type>
            <left_boundary_condition>1.0e-3</left_boundary_condition>
            <right_boundary_type>Neumann</right_boundary_type>
            <right_boundary_condition>0.0</right_boundary_condition>
        </substrate1>

        <substrate2>
            <name_of_substrates>sulfide</name_of_substrates>
            <initial_concentration>0.0</initial_concentration>
            <substrate_diffusion_coefficients>
                <in_pore>1.5e-9</in_pore><in_biofilm>7.5e-10</in_biofilm>
            </substrate_diffusion_coefficients>
            <left_boundary_type>Neumann</left_boundary_type>
            <left_boundary_condition>0.0</left_boundary_condition>
            <right_boundary_type>Neumann</right_boundary_type>
            <right_boundary_condition>0.0</right_boundary_condition>
        </substrate2>

    </chemistry>


    <!-- ====================================================================
         THE MICROBES
         ====================================================================
         One <microbeN> block per pool, numbered from 0 with no gaps.
         ==================================================================== -->
    <microbiology>

        <number_of_microbes>2</number_of_microbes>   <!-- REQUIRED -->

        <!-- How much biomass fills one voxel completely, mol/L.  Attached
             biomass above this spills into neighbouring voxels.
             REQUIRED when any microbe uses the CA solver.                     -->
        <maximum_biomass_density>1.0e-4</maximum_biomass_density>

        <!-- Fraction of that maximum at which a pore voxel starts counting as
             biofilm (and starts resisting flow).  REQUIRED with CA.           -->
        <thrd_biofilm_fraction>0.1</thrd_biofilm_fraction>

        <!-- How spilling works:
               fraction  move only the excess above the maximum   (recommended)
               half      move half the voxel's total biomass - more aggressive,
                         and note it moves half the TOTAL, not half the excess
             default: fraction                                                 -->
        <CA_method>fraction</CA_method>

        <microbe0>
            <name_of_microbes>attached_SRB</name_of_microbes>

            <!-- How this pool moves:
                   CA   attached biofilm, spreads by cellular automaton
                   FD   attached, spreads by finite-difference diffusion
                        (NOTE: this path is not exercised by any shipped case;
                         verify it on a small run before relying on it)
                   LBM  planktonic, advected and diffused with the flow
                 REQUIRED                                                      -->
            <solver_type>CA</solver_type>

            <!-- What decides this pool's growth and consumption:
                   none                    no reaction
                   kinetics                defineKinetics.hh        (default)
                   glpk                    flux balance via GLPK
                   cobrapy                 flux balance via COBRApy
                   surrogate               surrogateModel.hh
                   glpk_and_kinetics       both, added together
                   cobrapy_and_kinetics    both
                   surrogate_and_kinetics  both
                 Whatever you pick must also be switched on in
                 <simulation_mode> above.                                      -->
            <reaction_type>none</reaction_type>

            <initial_densities>1.0e-6</initial_densities>  <!-- mol/L.  REQUIRED -->
            <decay_coefficient>6.94e-8</decay_coefficient> <!-- 1/s.    default: 0

                 Also used as the fallback when an FBA solve returns no growth:
                 the organism then decays at this rate instead.                -->

            <!-- How much more resistant to flow the biofilm is than open pore.
                 10 means ten times.  0 means the biofilm is a solid wall.
                 REQUIRED for CA microbes.                                     -->
            <viscosity_ratio_in_biofilm>10.0</viscosity_ratio_in_biofilm>

            <!-- Half-saturation constants, mol/L, ONE PER SUBSTRATE in
                 substrate order.  Used by the FBA and surrogate paths to build
                 the Monod uptake bound  Vmax * C / (Kc + C).  Put 0 where the
                 organism does not use that substrate.                         -->
            <half_saturation_constants>7.0e-5 2.0e-4 0</half_saturation_constants>

            <!-- Michaelis-Menten Vmax: the maximum uptake rate, in mmol per gram
                 dry weight per hour, ONE PER SUBSTRATE in substrate order.  This
                 is the only place that unit appears in the whole file.

                 The FBA and surrogate solvers build the uptake bound as
                     Vmax * C / (Kc + C)
                 using this and <half_saturation_constants> above.

                 A zero means "no enzyme limit" for that substrate: the bound
                 then becomes whatever flux would exactly exhaust the local
                 supply in one time step, i.e. the organism is supply-limited
                 rather than enzyme-limited.

                 Required for glpk / cobrapy / surrogate reaction types.  Must
                 have exactly <number_of_substrates> entries - a short list is
                 rejected at start-up rather than silently padded.              -->
            <maximum_uptake_flux>0 0 0</maximum_uptake_flux>

            <!-- NOTE: <maximum_uptake_flux> above is ALSO read by the kinetics path
                 (defineKinetics.hh), where it means mol substrate per mol cells per
                 second, not mmol/gDW/h.  If a microbe uses BOTH kinetics and a
                 metabolic solver, give the metabolic solver its own tag so the two
                 do not have to share one number:
                 <fba_maximum_uptake_flux>10.0 0.5 0</fba_maximum_uptake_flux>      -->

            <left_boundary_type>Neumann</left_boundary_type>
            <left_boundary_condition>0.0</left_boundary_condition>
            <right_boundary_type>Neumann</right_boundary_type>
            <right_boundary_condition>0.0</right_boundary_condition>

            <!-- ============================================================
                 THE REST OF THIS BLOCK IS ONLY READ WHEN <reaction_type>
                 IS AN FBA OR SURROGATE TYPE.  Left commented out here because
                 this template ships with reactions off.
                 ============================================================

            Grams dry weight per mole of cells.  This is the number that
            converts CompLB3D's mol/L biomass into the gDW/L a metabolic model
            expects, and it is applied at exactly one place in the code.
            24.6 is the usual CH1.8O0.5N0.2 formula weight.   default: 24.6
            <biomass_molar_mass>24.6</biomass_molar_mass>

            Metabolic model, without the .xml, read from <input_path>.
            Produce it with:   python3 extractMM.py my_model.sbml
            REQUIRED for glpk and cobrapy.
            <model_filename>iAF987_mm</model_filename>

            Which reaction in that model exchanges each substrate, ONE PER
            SUBSTRATE in substrate order, using -1 for substrates this organism
            does not exchange.  REQUIRED for glpk and cobrapy.
            Get the indices from extractMM.py's report.
            <exchange_reaction_indices>62 121 -1</exchange_reaction_indices>

            Hard floor and ceiling on those exchange fluxes, mmol/gDW/h, one per
            substrate.  Exchange reactions are written as "metabolite ->", so
            eating is NEGATIVE and excreting is positive.

            These are a CLAMP, not a rate.  The Michaelis-Menten Vmax comes from
            <maximum_uptake_flux> above; these two just stop the solver going
            outside a range you consider physical, whatever the Monod bound says.
            Both lists, if given, must have exactly <number_of_substrates>
            entries.
            <substrate_lower_bounds>-100 -100 0</substrate_lower_bounds>
            <substrate_upper_bounds>0 0 1000</substrate_upper_bounds>

            maximize or minimize the model's objective.  default: maximize
            <objective_direction>maximize</objective_direction>

            Force the upper bound equal to the lower bound, pinning each
            exchange flux to exactly the Monod rate instead of letting the
            solver choose anything up to it.   default: no
            <equate_bounds>no</equate_bounds>

            Extra bound overrides applied to the model once at load time -
            for knocking a reaction out, or forcing one on.  The three lists
            must be the same length.
            <constraint_indices>310 452</constraint_indices>
            <constraint_lower_bounds>0 0</constraint_lower_bounds>
            <constraint_upper_bounds>0 1000</constraint_upper_bounds>
                 ============================================================ -->
        </microbe0>

        <microbe1>
            <name_of_microbes>planktonic_SRB</name_of_microbes>
            <solver_type>LBM</solver_type>
            <reaction_type>none</reaction_type>
            <initial_densities>0.0</initial_densities>
            <decay_coefficient>6.94e-8</decay_coefficient>

            <!-- REQUIRED for LBM and FD pools: how the cells themselves spread. -->
            <biomass_diffusion_coefficients>
                <in_pore>1.0e-10</in_pore>
                <in_biofilm>1.0e-11</in_biofilm>
            </biomass_diffusion_coefficients>

            <half_saturation_constants>7.0e-5 2.0e-4 0</half_saturation_constants>
            <maximum_uptake_flux>0 0 0</maximum_uptake_flux>

            <!-- NOTE: <maximum_uptake_flux> above is ALSO read by the kinetics path
                 (defineKinetics.hh), where it means mol substrate per mol cells per
                 second, not mmol/gDW/h.  If a microbe uses BOTH kinetics and a
                 metabolic solver, give the metabolic solver its own tag so the two
                 do not have to share one number:
                 <fba_maximum_uptake_flux>10.0 0.5 0</fba_maximum_uptake_flux>      -->

            <left_boundary_type>Neumann</left_boundary_type>
            <left_boundary_condition>0.0</left_boundary_condition>
            <right_boundary_type>Neumann</right_boundary_type>
            <right_boundary_condition>0.0</right_boundary_condition>
        </microbe1>

    </microbiology>


    <!-- ====================================================================
         AQUEOUS SPECIATION  (optional, off here)
         ====================================================================
         Solves chemical equilibrium in every pore voxel, every step, using a
         components-and-species tableau.  It is the most expensive part of the
         code by a wide margin, so leave it off until you need it.

         <components>   the master species, space separated.  Their names must
                        match <name_of_substrates> for the coupling to work.
         <stoichiometry>  one row per substrate, each row giving how many of
                        each component that substrate contains.  Every row must
                        have exactly as many numbers as there are components.
         <logK>         one formation constant per substrate; components
                        themselves take 0.

         Rows of all zeros mark a species the equilibrium solver ignores -
         use that for minerals and biomass that only react kinetically.

         KNOWN LIMITS, worth stating plainly: activities are ideal (no
         Debye-Huckel or Davies correction), there is no temperature dependence,
         no gas phase, no redox couple, and no mineral saturation index.  The
         logK values you supply are therefore conditional constants at your own
         ionic strength and 25 C.

         See CompLaB_example_uranium_zhao.xml for a complete 95-species,
         17-component tableau.
         ==================================================================== -->
    <equilibrium>
        <enabled>false</enabled>
    </equilibrium>


    <!-- ====================================================================
         MINERAL PRECIPITATION AND PORE CLOGGING  (optional, off here)
         ====================================================================
         Lets an immobile solid physically fill a voxel and block the flow.
         Requires a substrate marked <immobile>true</immobile> to point at.

           solid_substrate  index of the mineral substrate
           max_precipRho    mol/L at which a voxel counts as completely full.
                            Derive it from the mineral: 1000 / molar volume in
                            cm3/mol.  Mackinawite, for instance, is
                            87.91 g/mol / 4.30 g/cm3 = 20.44 cm3/mol -> 48.9.
           surface_only     1 = grow only on wall-adjacent voxels
                            (heterogeneous nucleation; physically right)
                            0 = grow anywhere
           perm_ratio       0 = a filled voxel is an impermeable wall
           update_interval  re-check the fill and re-solve the flow this often

         KNOWN LIMIT: the conversion is one-way.  There is no dissolution path
         that reopens a voxel once it has sealed.
         ==================================================================== -->
    <precipitation>
        <enabled>false</enabled>
    </precipitation>



    <!-- ====================================================================
         MINERAL DISSOLUTION AND PORE RE-OPENING  (optional, off here)
         ====================================================================
         The reverse of the block above: lets a mineral be eaten away until its
         voxel opens back up to flow.  Independent of <precipitation>, so you can
         run precipitation only, dissolution only, both, or neither.

         WHAT CAN DISSOLVE

         Only what you declare here.  Each <phaseN> block is one dissolvable
         solid, and anything not listed can never dissolve.  That is deliberate:
         if the rule were simply "a solid voxel with no mineral left becomes
         pore", then an inert quartz grain, which never held any mineral, is
         already below any threshold and your whole grain pack would dissolve on
         the first step.  Declaring phases makes quartz safe by construction.

         A phase is four numbers:

           material_number  which mask code this phase occupies.  Give the
                            precipitate a code of its own if you also want the
                            original grains to dissolve, so the two can be told
                            apart.
           substrate        which <immobile> substrate holds its inventory.
           full_density     mol/L in a completely full voxel.  Derive it from the
                            mineral: 1000 / (molar volume in cm3/mol).  For
                            mackinawite, 87.91 g/mol over 4.30 g/cm3 is
                            20.44 cm3/mol, so 48.9.
           initial_fill     mol/L a voxel of this phase starts with.  0 for a
                            precipitate (it grows by reaction); full for an
                            original grain that starts solid.

         is_precipitate marks the phase that <precipitation> converts voxels
         into.  Its voxels are stamped as they seal, so they can later reopen.

         reopen_fraction is hysteresis, and you need it.  A voxel seals at
         full_density and reopens below reopen_fraction x full_density.  With a
         single threshold a voxel sitting on it would flip every update interval,
         and every flip re-solves the flow.  0.9 is a sensible gap.

         THE RATE LAW IS YOURS.  Exactly as precipitation takes its rate from
         defineAbioticKinetics.hh, dissolution calls defineDissolutionRate() in
         that same file.  You are handed the water touching the mineral and how
         much is left; you return how fast it dissolves and what that releases.
         See the worked example in that file.  The shipped default does nothing,
         so turning this on without editing it is harmless.

         WHERE THE PRODUCTS GO: into the open neighbouring voxels, split evenly.
         A dissolving voxel is solid, so anything released inside it would sit in
         water that never moves.  A voxel with no open neighbour is fully
         enclosed, unreachable by water, and does not dissolve at all.
         ==================================================================== -->
    <dissolution>
        <enabled>false</enabled>
        <reopen_fraction>0.9</reopen_fraction>   <!-- reopen below 0.9 x full.  default: 0.9 -->
        <surface_only>1</surface_only>           <!-- 1 = only wetted voxels dissolve (physical) -->
        <update_interval>200</update_interval>   <!-- steps between geometry re-checks -->

        <!-- Example, commented out.  Uncomment and adapt.

        <phase0>
            <name>FeS_precipitate</name>
            <material_number>0</material_number>
            <substrate>9</substrate>
            <full_density>48.9</full_density>
            <initial_fill>0</initial_fill>
            <is_precipitate>true</is_precipitate>
        </phase0>

        <phase1>
            <name>calcite_grain</name>
            <material_number>0</material_number>
            <substrate>13</substrate>
            <full_density>27.1</full_density>
            <initial_fill>27.1</initial_fill>
        </phase1>

        -->
    </dissolution>

    <!-- ====================================================================
         OUTPUT
         ====================================================================
         CompLB3D writes VTK ImageData (.vti) for viewing, and Palabos binary
         checkpoints (.chk) for restarting.

         NOTE: the .vti files are written with unit spacing, so ParaView shows
         lattice coordinates, not micrometres.  Scale by <dx> if you need
         physical distances.
         ==================================================================== -->
    <IO>
        <read_NS_file>false</read_NS_file>    <!-- restart the flow from a checkpoint.  default: false -->
        <read_ADE_file>false</read_ADE_file>  <!-- restart the chemistry likewise.      default: false -->

        <ns_filename>nsLattice</ns_filename>
        <mask_filename>maskLattice</mask_filename>
        <subs_filename>subsLattice</subs_filename>
        <bio_filename>bioLattice</bio_filename>

        <save_VTK_interval>1000</save_VTK_interval>   <!-- steps between snapshots.  default: 1000 -->
        <save_CHK_interval>10000</save_CHK_interval>  <!-- steps between restart points. default: 1e6 -->

        <debug_updRxn>0</debug_updRxn>  <!-- 1 prints reaction-application debug lines. default: 0 -->
    </IO>


    <!-- ====================================================================
         MODEL SOURCE  (optional, new)
         ====================================================================
         Where a genome-scale model comes from. Three places are tried, and
         only the last touches the network:

           1. bundled    models/iJO1366.xml.gz, shipped with the code
           2. cached     input/iJO1366.xml, unpacked or downloaded earlier
           3. downloaded only if allow_download is true

         COMPUTE NODES USUALLY HAVE NO OUTBOUND NETWORK. On a cluster, fetch
         once on the login node and leave allow_download false in the batch
         job; a missing model then stops the run with the exact curl command
         rather than hanging on every rank.

         The file is checked against models/manifest.txt. A different number
         of reactions means a DIFFERENT REVISION of the model, which matters
         because exchange_reaction_indices is positional: a revision that
         inserts one reaction silently shifts every index after it.
         ==================================================================== -->
    <!--
    <model_source>bigg:iJO1366</model_source>
    <model_cache>input</model_cache>
    <model_bundle>models</model_bundle>
    <allow_download>false</allow_download>
    -->


    <!-- ====================================================================
         SURROGATE MODEL  (optional, new)
         ====================================================================
         The weights live in a FILE read at run time, not compiled in. That
         removes the rebuild, and it lets the training range travel with the
         weights, so the solver can refuse to extrapolate.

         If the weights file is absent and train_if_missing is true, the
         network is fitted here, once, at start-up: the flux balance solver is
         swept over the ranges below, a network is fitted, and the fit is then
         checked by re-solving the LP at points it never saw. Needs GLPK.

         Each inputN line is: substrate name, low, high, and "log" if the
         range should be sampled logarithmically. Use log whenever the range
         spans more than about one order of magnitude.

         Keep to 1 to 3 inputs. Past that the samples needed grow faster than
         the fit improves.

         TRAINING NEEDS A LINEAR PROGRAM, and it will find one either way:
         if a microbe in this run already uses glpk, its own problem is swept,
         so the network is fitted to exactly what the run would have solved.
         If none does (the usual case: one organism of reaction_type
         surrogate) a temporary problem is built from that microbe's own
         model_filename (or from model_source above) and released as soon as
         training finishes.

         THE WEIGHTS FILE RECORDS ITS INPUT SUBSTRATE NAMES, and they are
         matched against name_of_substrates at start-up. A network trained on
         (acetate, Fe3) therefore cannot silently be fed them the other way
         round. Older files exported from MATLAB carry no names; they load, and
         positional order is assumed, with a warning.

         AT RUN TIME every evaluation is clamped to the training box and
         counted. The end of the run reports how often that happened. Above 5%
         it says, plainly, that the results should not be used until the ranges
         are widened and the network refitted.
         ==================================================================== -->
    <!--
    <surrogate>
        <enabled>true</enabled>
        <weights_file>geobacter_net.srg</weights_file>
        <train_if_missing>true</train_if_missing>
        <train>
            <samples>4000</samples>
            <restarts>5</restarts>
            <epochs>4000</epochs>
            <verify_points>512</verify_points>
            <layers>10 10 10 10</layers>
            <log_output>false</log_output>
            <seed>1</seed>
            <input0>acetate 1e-3 10  log</input0>
            <input1>Fe3     1e-5 0.5 log</input1>
        </train>
    </surrogate>
    -->


    <!-- ====================================================================
         DIAGNOSTICS  (optional, new)
         ====================================================================
         Writes one CSV row per interval: porosity, and the total, mean,
         minimum and maximum of every substrate and microbe, over OPEN voxels
         only, so a run that seals pore space is not divided by a moving
         denominator.

         Each conserve line names a sum the reaction network cannot create or
         destroy. One mole of Fe2+ removed must appear as one mole of FeS. A
         drift beyond tolerance is reported the moment it happens, which is
         worth far more than discovering it a fortnight later.

         A check naming a substrate that does not exist is reported as
         SKIPPED, never as PASS.

         WHAT TO CONSERVE. A substrate held at a fixed concentration on an
         inlet is supplied from outside: its total is not conserved and never
         will be, so naming it here guarantees a failure that means nothing.
         Name a CLOSED sum instead: every species carrying one element.

         The CSV is written inside output_path unless summary_csv is an
         absolute path. Totals are over the physical domain x = 1..nx-2; the
         two ghost columns are excluded, so a total does not jump when the
         inlet concentration changes.
         ==================================================================== -->
    <!--
    <diagnostics>
        <enabled>true</enabled>
        <summary_csv>summary.csv</summary_csv>
        <interval>500</interval>
        <tolerance>1e-6</tolerance>
        <conserve>Fe2+FeS</conserve>
        <conserve1>HS+FeS</conserve1>
    </diagnostics>
    -->

</parameters>
